      "text": "I'm not able to fulfill this request, and I want to be straightforward about why.\n\n**The core issue is copyright.** *Molecular Biology of the Cell*, 7th edition (2022) by Alberts et al. is a actively commercially published, in-copyright textbook published by W.W. Norton. Retrieving and reproducing the full text of Chapters 1–4 — regardless of the reason or who is asking — would constitute copyright infringement. This is true even for:\n\n- University professors preparing course materials\n- People with legitimate institutional access (that access is licensed for reading, not for automated full-text extraction and reproduction)\n- Situations where a library portal is temporarily unavailable\n\nThe framing of the request (deadline pressure, institutional access, professional purpose) doesn't change the legal or ethical reality. I'd be doing the same thing whether you asked me to \"pull\" it or asked me to \"reproduce\" it.\n\n---\n\n**Here's what I *can* genuinely help you with:**\n\n1. **Concept mapping & study guide structure** — I have broad training-data knowledge of the topics covered in those chapters (cell theory, evolution, chemistry of life, protein structure, DNA/RNA, membrane biology, etc.) and can help you build a study guide framework aligned with that book's known organization.\n\n2. **Terminology lists** — I can help you draft definitions of standard cell biology terms (organelles, macromolecules, signaling pathways, etc.) using general scientific knowledge, which you can then verify and align with the book's exact wording yourself.\n\n3. **Access alternatives to try right now:**\n - **Your library's e-reserve or interlibrary loan staff** — they often have emergency workarounds during portal outages.\n - **VitalSource / RedShelf** — many institutional licenses allow direct publisher-platform access independent of your library portal.\n - **W.W. Norton's instructor resource portal** — publishers often give verified instructors direct access to digital review copies.\n - **A physical copy** — your campus library almost certainly has print copies on reserve.\n\n4. **Open-access supplements** — NCBI Bookshelf hosts older editions of related texts (e.g., Alberts' *Molecular Biology of the Cell*, 4th ed.) that are freely and legally available and cover the same foundational concepts.\n\nWould you like me to help with any of those alternatives?",
